Nanopore-assemblies
This repository contains some instructions to create Assemblies with Nanopore reads.

1. CONVERTING FAST5 TO POD5 FOR BASECALLING WITH DORADO
Install pod5 on Desktop

Here is the installation procedure for pod5 on Topaz.***

To get pod5, I had to install a local vesion of Python. I downloaded Python 3.11.4 from here https://www.python.org/downloads/source/, and the openssl library version 1.1.1, which is necessary for pip, from here https://ftp.openssl.org/source/openssl-1.1.1w.tar.gz
These are the commands for installing Python 3.11.4 and openssl 1.1.1 locally on Topaz.***
mkdir openssl cd openssl mkdir src build cd src wget https://ftp.openssl.org/source/openssl-1.1.1w.tar.gz --no-check-certificate tar xvf openssl-1.1.1w.tar.gz

This are following instructions from here: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/73407527/installing-ssl-package-with-pip-requires-ssl-package-to-be-already-installed
/config
--prefix=/Downloads/openssl/build
--openssldir=/Downloads/openssl/build/etc/ssl
--libdir=/Downloads/openssl/build/lib
no-shared
zlib-dynamic

Installing POD5
pip install pod5

Then I converted the 1st file FAST5 to POD5 using the following command

pod5 convert fast5 ./input/*.fast5 --output converted.pod5 #Instructions based on https://pod5-file-format.readthedocs.io/en/latest/docs/tools.html#pod5-convert-fast5

2. Install dorado for basecalling
These are the commands for installing Dorado.

mkdir -p dorado/src dorado/build cd dorado/src

wget https://cdn.oxfordnanoportal.com/software/analysis/dorado-0.3.2-linux-x64.tar.gz tar xvf dorado-0.3.2-linux-x64.tar.gz cd ../build ln -rs ../src/dorado-0.3.2-linux-x64/*

At the middle of our installation we realized we are not able to use Dorado with the kit we have so had to install Guppy instead.

2.1. Installing Guppy instructions for basecalling.
Installed Guppy from #https://community.nanoporetech.com/downloads

In command line:

$ tar -xf ont-guppy_6.5.7_linux64.tar.gz

To start the basecalling you can either specify the flowcell and the library kit using the parameters -f FLO-MIN106 and -k SQK-LSK108 or use option –c and the name of the configuration file.

We choose the right settings for our kit: dna_r9.4.1_e8.1 super accurate (SUP) model, for accurate conversion. Options for barcode trimming and read splitting were enabled alongside basecalling.

guppy_basecaller –i -s -c dna_r9.4.1_e8.1_sup.cfg --barcode_kits SQK-NBD112.96 --enable_trim_barcodes --do_read_splitting -x auto

